Replace the macOS-only `open` with a cross-platform artifact opener

Upstream's only "show the user the artifact" instruction is
`Bash(open path/to/show-me-{description}.html)` -- macOS's `open`
command, with no fallback for any other platform. Upstream's own history
(d3138e5, "open HTML with Bash instead of task-artifact") shows they
deliberately moved away from a HumanLayer-product-specific inline-display
mechanism to this generic Bash(open ...) precisely so the skill would
work as a portable skill across different coding agents -- the same
reasoning behind this change, one step further.

Adds scripts/open_artifact.py: detects the actual environment (native
Windows, WSL, Linux desktop/headless, macOS desktop/headless,
devcontainer) and only attempts an opener that's genuinely available
there (shutil.which-gated, never assumed). A missing opener never loses
the artifact, hangs, or gets retried -- the file and its real path are
the result either way. 23 unit tests, all pure/injectable (no mocking of
global state).

Also adds LICENSE.txt (MIT, HumanLayer (c) 2026, exact upstream text) and
a fork note in SKILL.md, matching the skill-creator fork's convention.
disable-model-invocation (Claude Code) and agents/openai.yaml (Codex)
are untouched from upstream. See vendor/show-me/DISPOSITION.md for the
known manual-invocation gap on OpenCode/Antigravity this does not fix.

